# Special Features: Redirects, Timeouts, and No-Follow

@baseUrl = https://api.example.com

###

### Default: Follow redirects
# By default, the extension follows HTTP redirects (301, 302, 303, 307, 308)
# Final response body and status is from the redirect target

GET https://example.com/old-endpoint

###

### Disable redirect following for this request
# Use # @no-follow to opt out of following redirects
# Response will have the redirect status (301/302) and Location header

# @no-follow
GET https://example.com/redirect-me

###

### Check response headers to follow manually
# If you disable follow, you can see:
#   Status: 301 Moved Permanently
#   Location: https://example.com/new-endpoint
# Then manually create a request to the new endpoint

# @no-follow
GET https://example.com/check-redirect

###

### Setting timeout (in VS Code settings)
# Configure in VS Code settings → Extensions → Http Client:
# dot-http.timeout: "30s"
#
# Supports: ms (milliseconds), s (seconds), m (minutes)
# Examples: "500ms", "30s", "5m"
# Empty or unset = no timeout

GET {{baseUrl}}/slow-endpoint

###

### Long-running request with timeout
# With timeout set to "30s" in settings:
# If this request takes longer than 30 seconds, it will be cancelled

# @expect 200
POST {{baseUrl}}/process-large-file
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "action": "process_all",
  "timeout_seconds": 25
}

###

### Very fast request with timeout
# Finishes before timeout, no issue

# @expect 200
GET {{baseUrl}}/health

###

### Request without timeout (empty setting)
# If dot-http.timeout is empty, requests wait indefinitely

GET {{baseUrl}}/long-running-operation

###

### Status code validation with redirects
# Assertion on final status after following redirects

# @expect 200
# @assert body.data != null
GET https://example.com/will-redirect-to-200

###

### Redirect to error page
# @no-follow
# @expect 301
GET https://example.com/redirect-to-404

###

### Combined: timeout + redirect + assertion
# @expect 200
# @assert body.success == true
# Request follows redirects automatically
# Must complete within timeout window

GET {{baseUrl}}/endpoint-that-redirects

###

### HTTP Status Codes and Redirects
# 301 Moved Permanently     — old URL no longer exists (cached redirect)
# 302 Found                 — temporary redirect
# 303 See Other             — redirect with GET (even if original was POST)
# 307 Temporary Redirect    — same method, not cached
# 308 Permanent Redirect    — same method, cached

# By default: all redirects followed automatically
# With @no-follow: stop and show redirect response

###

### Timeout during redirect chain
# If first request redirects to second URL,
# and timeout fires during the chain,
# you'll see a timeout error

# @expect 200
GET https://example.com/multi-redirect

###

### No-follow useful for:
# 1. Testing redirect behavior
# 2. Checking Location header values
# 3. Debugging redirect chains
# 4. Security testing (ensure redirect goes to safe URL)
# 5. API contracts (some APIs require following redirects yourself)

###

### Timeout useful for:
# 1. Setting SLA boundaries (API must respond in 30s)
# 2. Preventing hung requests in CI/CD
# 3. Load testing (measure time-to-first-byte)
# 4. Testing timeout handling in client code

###

### Configuration precedence:
# 1. @no-follow in request (highest)
# 2. dot-http.followRedirects in settings (default: true)
# 3. HTTP client follow default (true)

# 1. Request-level timeout (future feature)
# 2. dot-http.timeout setting
# 3. No timeout (infinite)
